- op: discuss  # 4113
  at: '2026-10-03T06:42:07Z'  # 4113
  by: agent  # 4113
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4113
  clock: 3  # 4113
  nonce: '4113'  # 4113
  message: "PREMISE AUDIT 2026-10-03 (owner-requested; read-only, reproduced at dev 3abedae588 on small fixtures). CURRENT, wider than titled. Title updated.\n- _process_sequential / _contains_classified_descendant (cfg.py:833-952) unchanged in substance since 2026-09-27.\n- Repro: Java int r = switch... and Runnable q = () -> { if ... } exactly as filed (r, q use-only; arm values absent; go(); inlined). TS const g = () => { if (c) go(); }: g use-only, body inlined (const v = c ? x : 0 is correct). RUST let x = if c..., let v = match..., let h = |y| { if ... }: x, v, h use-only; match arms split; closure body inlined. GO (not named before) h := func() { if c { go1() } }: h use-only, body inlined. Python and C ternaries are correct.\n- WIDER THAN DECLARATIONS: a callback passed inside an ordinary expression statement, xs.forEach(x => { if (x) { sink(t); } }) in TS and Java, splits into xs, forEach, x, (x) and an inlined sink(t); the forEach call itself is never a statement. Callback arguments are probably more common than lambdas assigned in declarations.\n- FIX CONSTRAINT: ADR-0017 line ~906 commits to a conservative over-approximation for closures (a tainted captured variable taints the closure). So the \"not at all\" option must keep the closure body's captured uses, recorded on the enclosing statement.\n- Related, not duplicates: WI-pohib (closure sink ordering), WI-rovun (done).\n- NOT CHECKED: C#/Kotlin (no def/use extractor); effect on real-repo verdicts."  # 4113
- op: discuss  # e6ea
  at: '2026-10-05T09:15:00Z'  # e6ea
  by: agent  # e6ea
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e6ea
  clock: 4  # e6ea
  nonce: e6ea  # e6ea
  message: "Fixed on branch jgstern-agent/fix/losod (same commits as WI-losod: 11a10f98b0, 445768867b, a9e3a869f4, 2c26be3459; not pushed). An atomic statement that holds control flow in a VALUE (let x = if/match .., int r = switch .., let k = f()?;) now processes that control flow first and is recorded ONCE after it, so it defines its target. A nested function is listed under a new cfg_nodes key, nested_function, and is not a classify() category. The statement holding one stays one statement. The function's body is built as its own region, with its own exit, loop stack and deferred list, that may run: it is entered from the statement, may repeat, and rejoins after it, while the statement's own fall-through is kept. This keeps ADR-0017's closure over-approximation: captured values are seen at the body's own lines, and a binding inside the body can no longer kill an outer definition. Also fixed: Java arrow-form switch_rule arms. Before, an arrow switch left every later statement unreachable. Rust closure parameters are no longer definitions of the enclosing function.\nBehavioral evidence: verify-claims, base c1dd831558 vs branch. Java `String w = switch ..`: not_attempted -> ddg/confirmed. Java Supplier lambda with an if: escaped -> ddg/confirmed. Rust let-if and let-match: escaped -> ddg/confirmed. Rust `let v = env::var(..)?;`: structural -> ddg/confirmed. Kill case, where a closure-local t used to hide the outer tainted t, in go, ts, python and rust: not_attempted -> ddg/confirmed. A sink inside a Java forEach callback stays ddg/confirmed. Mutation control: dropping the region's skip edge sends the four kill fixtures back to escaped. Corpus: ansible _cmd_help sys.argv -> print moved from cross_function to ddg/confirmed. Two nested defs there no longer wire their `return` to the outer function's exit. No verdict moved on any of the 6 repos.\nNOT fixed: `h := func(){..v..}; sink(h())` stays escaped in go/ts/rust, because the callee name is not counted as a use. Filed as WI-bufar-riban-titit-kujon-nonoh-zovut-duhap-gadik. Also found and filed: switch has no \"no case matched\" edge (pre-existing, P2), WI-sozob-robof-folub-sujit-gopov-bisoz-pirub-norom."  # e6ea
